from author import Bank,save_batch
banks=[]
b=Bank('topic-25','digital','boolean-algebra','Boolean Algebra · Cofactors, Parity & Identities','008');banks.append(b)
b.m(r'Which expression is equivalent to F=A+AB for every Boolean A,B?','C',['B','AB','A','A XOR B'],'Boolean OR as +; AND as multiplication','Simplify F.','Absorption law',r'A+AB=A(1+B)=A',r'Factor A using distributivity. | In Boolean algebra 1+B=1 for either value of B. | Thus F=A, option C.','Boolean identities use idempotent OR rather than arithmetic addition.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'For F=A XOR B XOR C, how many of the eight three-bit input words produce F=1?',4,'Three-input odd-parity function','Count its high truth-table rows.','Parity population',r'F=1\iff A+B+C\text{ is odd}',r'One-high words contribute C(3,1)=3 cases. | The all-high word contributes one more odd-count case. | Total high rows are 3+1=4.','Three-input XOR means odd parity, not exactly one input high.')
b.s(r'Which expressions are identically equal to NOT(A OR B)?',['A','D'],['(NOT A) AND (NOT B)','(NOT A) OR (NOT B)','A AND B','NOT A AND NOT B AND 1'],'Two-input Boolean functions','Select equivalent De Morgan forms.','Complement of a sum',r'\overline{A+B}=\bar A\bar B',r'De Morgan’s law changes OR to AND and complements both inputs. | Multiplying the resulting expression by 1 does not change it. | Therefore A and D are equivalent, whereas B complements an AND instead and C uses uninverted inputs.','Complement both inputs and reverse the logical operator.')
b.n(r'For F(A,B,C)=AB+ĀC, find the number of input words on which the cofactor F|A=1 equals 1, counting only free inputs B,C.',2,'A fixed to 1; B,C remain Boolean','Count high rows of the two-variable cofactor.','Shannon cofactor',r'F|_{A=1}=B',r'Setting A=1 eliminates ĀC and leaves B. | B is 1 for (B,C)=(1,0),(1,1). | There are 2 high rows over the four free-input combinations.','Once a variable is fixed, do not count both values of it.')
b.m(r'The dual of F=A+BC, formed by swapping AND and OR and interchanging constants 0 and 1, is:','B',['Ā(B̄+C̄)','A(B+C)','A+ B+ C','AB+C'],'Boolean algebraic duality; no constants present','Find the dual without complementing variables.','Boolean duality',r'+\ \leftrightarrow\ \cdot',r'The outer OR becomes AND. | The inner product BC becomes the sum B+C. | The dual is A(B+C), option B. Duality does not complement the variables.','Dual and complement are different operations.')
b.s(r'Which are true Boolean identities?',['A','C','D'],['A XOR A = 0','A XOR 0 = 0','A XOR 1 = NOT A','A AND (B OR C) = (A AND B) OR (A AND C)'],'Ordinary two-valued Boolean algebra','Select all identities.','XOR and distributivity',r'A\oplus A=0,\quad A\oplus1=\bar A',r'Equal bits have zero XOR; XOR with zero preserves the input, so B is false. | XOR with one complements a bit. | AND distributes over OR. Select A, C and D.','An XOR identity cannot be substituted for an AND or OR identity indiscriminately.')
b.n(r'For F=(A+B)(A+B̄), how many input combinations of A,B produce F=1?',2,'Product of two Boolean sums','Simplify then count high rows.','Consensus-style product identity',r'(A+B)(A+\bar B)=A+B\bar B=A',r'Expand or apply (X+Y)(X+Z)=X+YZ. | Since BB̄=0, the function reduces to A. | A=1 permits either B value, giving 2 high rows.','A product of sums is not evaluated by adding truth values as integers.')
b.n(r'Find the number of input words for which (A XOR B) AND C equals 1.',2,'Three Boolean inputs; odd parity of A,B gated by C','Count satisfying assignments.','Constraint factorization',r'F=1\iff A\ne B\text{ and }C=1',r'A,B must be (0,1) or (1,0). | C is then fixed to 1. | There are exactly 2 satisfying three-bit words.','The enable input C reduces the parity count by fixing one degree of freedom.')
b.m(r'For F=AB+ĀC+BC, which term is redundant under the consensus theorem?','D',['AB','ĀC','All three','BC'],'Three-product SOP; usual complemented A term','Identify the removable consensus term.','Consensus theorem',r'XY+\bar XZ+YZ=XY+\bar XZ',r'If B=C=1, then either A=1 makes AB=1 or A=0 makes ĀC=1. | Thus every high case covered by BC is already covered by another product. | Removing BC leaves the same truth table, so D is correct.','Logically redundant terms can still be useful for hazard suppression in a physical implementation.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'Given F(A,B)=ĀB+AB̄+AB, find the number of zero rows in its truth table.',1,'Three minterms cover all input words except one','Count zeros after simplification.','Minterm coverage',r'F=A+B',r'The three products cover input pairs 01,10,11. | Only 00 makes all products zero. | The number of zero rows is 1.','An OR of minterms counts covered rows, not the number of product symbols.',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'For any Boolean F with variable A, which Shannon-expansion statements hold?',['A','B','D'],['F=A F|A=1 + Ā F|A=0','The two cofactors can be implemented as mux data inputs selected by A','F must equal the XOR of its cofactors without any selection variable','If the two cofactors are identical, F is independent of A'],'Arbitrary finite Boolean function','Select correct cofactor consequences.','Shannon expansion',r'F=A F_1+\bar A F_0',r'For A=1 only the F1 branch survives, and for A=0 only F0 survives. | This is the behavior of a 2:1 multiplexer. | Identical cofactors remove dependence on A; their unselected XOR is not generally F. Select A, B and D.','Cofactors are functions of the remaining variables and must be selected appropriately.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'For the two-bit Boolean function F=A OR B, find the Hamming distance between its truth-table output vector and that of G=A AND B, using input order 00,01,10,11.',2,'Output vectors for OR and AND under common input ordering','Count positions at which outputs differ.','Boolean truth-vector distance',r'd_H(F,G)=\#\{x:F(x)\ne G(x)\}',r'OR has vector (0,1,1,1), while AND has (0,0,0,1). | They differ on inputs 01 and 10 only. | Their truth-vector Hamming distance is 2.','This distance compares function output vectors, not the input bit strings.')

b=Bank('topic-26','digital','logic-gates','Logic Gates · CMOS Networks & Noise Margins','008');banks.append(b)
b.m(r'A complementary static CMOS gate has two NMOS transistors in series in the pull-down network and the dual PMOS network. Its function is:','A',['NAND','NOR','AND without an inverter','XOR'],'Two-input conventional complementary CMOS; NMOS controlled by A,B','Identify the output logic function.','CMOS pull-down interpretation',r'Y=\overline{AB}',r'A path to ground exists only if both A and B are high. | The output is then low; for the other input states the dual pull-up makes it high. | This is NAND, option A.','The pull-down conduction condition is the complement of the output-high function.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A conventional static CMOS two-input NOR uses a complementary pull-up/pull-down pair of networks. How many MOS transistors are required, excluding output buffers?',4,'One PMOS and one NMOS per independent input in the conventional two-input NOR topology','Count transistors in both networks.','CMOS NOR topology',r'N_{MOS}=N_{NMOS}+N_{PMOS}',r'The pull-down has two parallel NMOS devices, one per input. | Its dual pull-up has two series PMOS devices. | Total count is 2+2=4, with no extra output buffer included.','The count is for the explicitly stated conventional topology, not every possible transistor logic family.')
b.n(r'A logic family specifies VOH,min=4.8 V and VIH,min=3.0 V. Find the high noise margin in volts.',1.8,'Guaranteed high output 4.8 V; required high input 3.0 V','Find NMH.','High-state noise margin',r'NM_H=V_{OH,min}-V_{IH,min}',r'The receiving input accepts a high down to 3.0 V. | The driving output guarantees at least 4.8 V. | Their separation gives high noise margin 4.8−3.0=1.8 V.','Use guaranteed minimum output-high voltage and minimum accepted input-high threshold.',unit='V')
b.n(r'For VOL,max=0.2 V and VIL,max=1.0 V, find the low noise margin in volts.',0.8,'Worst-case low output 0.2 V; maximum recognized low input 1.0 V','Find NML.','Low-state noise margin',r'NM_L=V_{IL,max}-V_{OL,max}',r'The receiving input remains low up to 1.0 V. | The driver’s worst-case low is 0.2 V. | The allowed positive disturbance is 1.0−0.2=0.8 V.','The subtraction order differs from the high-margin formula.',unit='V')
b.s(r'For ideal complementary static CMOS with no leakage and stable rail-to-rail inputs, which statements hold?',['A','C','D'],['There is no steady DC path between supply rails in a valid static state','Both pull-up and pull-down must conduct strongly in every stable state','Charging capacitance consumes dynamic energy when the output switches','The pull-up and pull-down implement complementary conduction conditions'],'Ideal static CMOS; leakage and transition overlap excluded from steady-state claim','Select valid static/dynamic power descriptions.','Complementary switching',r'P_{dyn}=\alpha C_LV_{DD}^2f',r'The complementary networks connect the settled output to one rail while breaking the path to the other. | Switching charges and discharges capacitance, consuming dynamic energy despite the ideal zero static conduction. | Thus A,C,D hold; simultaneous strong conduction is not the desired stable state.','Zero ideal static power is not zero switching power.')
b.n(r'Using P=αCVDD²f with α defined as the number of 0→1 charging events per clock cycle, C=10 fF, VDD=1.2 V, f=100 MHz and α=0.2, find dynamic power in μW.',0.288,'Specified charging-event activity; C=10⁻¹⁴ F; f=10⁸ Hz','Find dynamic switching power.','CMOS capacitive power',r'P=\alpha C V_{DD}^2f',r'VDD²=1.44 V² and C f=10⁻⁶ F/s. | Multiplying by α=0.2 gives 2.88×10⁻⁷ W. | This is 0.288 μW under the explicitly given activity convention.','Activity conventions can differ by a factor of two; use the charging-event definition supplied.',unit='μW')
b.m(r'A static CMOS pull-down network conducts when (A OR B) AND C is 1. The dual pull-up network is correctly implemented. What is output Y?','C',['(A OR B) AND C','A OR (B AND C)','NOT[(A OR B) AND C]','A XOR B XOR C'],'Complementary static CMOS; specified pull-down conduction','Find output-high Boolean condition.','Complex gate inversion',r'Y=\overline{(A+B)C}',r'When the pull-down condition is true, the output is connected to ground. | The complementary pull-up covers the remaining valid input states. | Thus the output is the complement of the conduction condition, option C.','A pull-down expression describes when Y is low, not when Y is high.')
b.n(r'In a gate’s conducting pull-down path, two identical ON NMOS switches are in series, each modeled by 2 kΩ. Find their total resistance in kΩ.',4,'Only one series path; ideal resistor ON models; each 2 kΩ','Find series ON resistance.','Gate switch-resistance model',r'R_{eq}=R_1+R_2',r'The same discharge current passes through both switches in series. | Their voltage drops add. | The path resistance is 2+2=4 kΩ in this stated simplified model.','Series transistor ON resistance is not the parallel combination.',unit='kΩ',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'Three identical ON switch branches of 6 kΩ each connect an output node to ground in parallel. Find the equivalent pull-down resistance in kΩ.',2,'Three equal parallel ON branches; no other paths','Find parallel path resistance.','Parallel CMOS discharge paths',r'G_{eq}=\sum_i1/R_i',r'Each branch contributes conductance 1/6 in reciprocal kΩ. | Three branches give total conductance 3/6=1/2. | The equivalent resistance is its reciprocal, 2 kΩ.','Multiple conducting parallel branches reduce the discharge resistance.',unit='kΩ')
b.s(r'Which two-input gate choices are functionally complete by themselves, allowing repeated instances and tied inputs as needed?',['A','D'],['NAND','AND','OR','NOR'],'Unrestricted composition of the chosen gate type; tied inputs permitted','Select universal gate primitives.','Functional completeness',r'\bar A=\operatorname{NAND}(A,A)=\operatorname{NOR}(A,A)',r'NAND can produce inversion, then AND and OR through De Morgan constructions. | NOR has the dual construction. | AND-only and OR-only circuits cannot produce inversion of a free input. Select A and D.','Completeness is about composing gates, not a single gate implementing every function directly.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A three-inverter chain has ideal Boolean input A. Find its output when A=1.',0,'Three cascaded ideal NOT gates','Propagate the bit through each stage.','Inversion parity',r'\bar{\bar{\bar A}}=\bar A',r'The first stage changes 1 to 0. | The second returns it to 1, and the third changes it to 0. | Output is 0; an odd number of inversions complements the input.','Count logic inversions rather than assuming every buffer chain preserves polarity.')
b.n(r'An AOI22 CMOS gate implements Y=NOT(AB+CD). Of the 16 input combinations, how many make Y=0?',7,'Four independent Boolean inputs; two AND terms feeding an inversion','Count the union of two conduction conditions.','Complex-gate inclusion–exclusion',r'\#(AB+CD)=\#(AB)+\#(CD)-\#(ABCD)',r'AB=1 fixes A,B and leaves C,D free, covering 4 words. CD=1 likewise covers 4. | The all-one word belongs to both sets and must be subtracted once. | The pull-down count is 4+4−1=7, so Y=0 on 7 words.','Counting the two products separately double-counts their overlap.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-27','digital','karnaugh-maps','Karnaugh Maps · Adjacency & Minimal Covers','008');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'For F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,1,2,3), with A the most significant minterm bit, find the minimum number of literals in a single-product SOP implementation.',1,'ON set 0,1,2,3; remaining minterms OFF; no don’t-cares','Find literal count after maximal grouping.','Four-cell K-map group',r'F=\bar A',r'The four ON cells cover all combinations of B,C while A remains 0. | Those varying variables disappear from the group term. | The result Ā is one product with one literal.','A minterm count is different from the number of literals after minimization.',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'The shown map has ON minterms 0 and 2 for variables A,B,C. What product covers exactly this adjacent pair?','C',['ĀB̄','AB','ĀC̄','BC'],'Three-variable map; Gray columns BC=00,01,11,10; A=0 for both ON cells','Identify the wraparound pair implicant.','K-map edge adjacency',r'm_0+m_2=\bar A\bar B\bar C+\bar AB\bar C=\bar A\bar C',r'The first and last map columns are adjacent in Gray-code topology. | Between minterms 000 and 010 only B changes. | Drop B and retain ĀC̄, option C.','Map edges wrap around; a rectangular display does not remove their adjacency.',diagram=('assets/diagrams/kmap-wrap.svg','K-map rows A=0,1 and Gray columns BC=00,01,11,10; ones only at minterms 0 and 2.'))
b.n(r'For F(A,B,C,D)=Σm(0,2,8,10), using ABCD as the binary minterm index, find the literal count of its maximal single implicant.',2,'Four-variable ON set 0,2,8,10','Identify fixed bits across the four cells.','Elimination of changing variables',r'F=\bar B\bar D',r'The four binary indices vary A and C independently. | B=0 and D=0 remain fixed in every cell. | The covering product is B̄D̄, with 2 literals.','List fixed bits using the declared minterm bit order.')
b.s(r'For F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,1,3,7), which listed prime implicants are essential?',['A','C'],['ĀB̄','ĀC','BC','AC'],'No don’t-cares; essential means covering an ON cell not covered by another prime implicant','Identify indispensable prime terms.','Essential prime implicants',r'\text{essential prime}\iff\text{uniquely covers an ON minterm}',r'The prime terms are ĀB̄ (0,1), ĀC (1,3) and BC (3,7). | Minterm 0 is covered only by ĀB̄, and minterm 7 only by BC. | Thus A and C are essential. AC is not even an implicant because it includes OFF minterm 5.','A large implicant is not automatically essential; unique coverage is the test.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'For F(A,B,C)=Σm(1,3) with don’t-care set d(5,7), find the fewest literals in an SOP implementing the specified ON/OFF requirements.',1,'ON 1,3; don’t-cares 5,7; all other cells OFF','Use allowed don’t-cares to enlarge a group.','Don’t-care minimization',r'F=C\quad\text{is valid on every specified cell}',r'The two ON minterms and both don’t-cares all have C=1. | Assigning the don’t-cares high permits a four-cell group. | The single literal C covers it while all specified OFF cells have C=0.','A don’t-care can be used if helpful; it is not a required ON cell.')
b.m(r'Which column order gives cyclic Gray adjacency for a two-bit K-map coordinate?','B',['00,01,10,11','00,01,11,10','00,11,01,10','00,10,01,11'],'Adjacent columns, including last-to-first, must differ in one bit','Choose a valid cyclic Gray order.','Gray coding',r'd_H(c_i,c_{i+1})=1',r'For B, consecutive words differ once: 00→01→11→10→00. | Each transition changes one bit, including the wrap. | Other listed orders have at least one two-bit transition. Select B.','Ordinary binary numerical order is not the cyclic Gray sequence.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A legal eight-cell implicant is formed in a four-variable K-map. How many variables remain as literals in that product?',1,'Four variables; legal 2³-cell group','Find the count of fixed variables.','Group size and literal count',r'L=n-\log_2g',r'An eight-cell group lets three variables vary independently. | Of four total variables, one remains fixed. | Its implicant therefore has one literal.','Only legal rectangular power-of-two groups have this elimination property.')
b.m(r'For F(A,B,C)=0 only at minterms 0 and 1, which minimal single sum term is correct?','A',['A+B','A+C','B+C','Ā+B̄'],'Other six cells ON; ABC minterm bit order','Find the POS sum covering the zero pair.','POS grouping of zeros',r'F=A+B',r'Zero cells 000 and 001 have A=B=0 while C varies. | A+B is zero exactly for those fixed A,B values. | It is one sum with two literals, option A.','For POS, group zeros and use the sum term whose zero pattern matches the group.')
b.s(r'Which K-map grouping rules are valid for ordinary two-level minimization?',['A','C','D'],['Legal groups contain a power-of-two number of adjacent cells','Diagonal touching alone makes two cells adjacent','Groups may overlap when useful','Groups may wrap across opposite map edges'],'Standard Gray-coded Karnaugh maps','Select valid adjacency and cover rules.','K-map geometry',r'g\in\{1,2,4,8,\ldots\}',r'Groups represent Boolean cubes, so their size is a power of two. | Overlap can help cover cells with fewer literals, and opposite edges are adjacent by Gray-code wrap. | Merely diagonal contact does not provide one-bit adjacency. Select A,C,D.','A visually close diagonal is not a legal two-cell Boolean cube.')
b.n(r'For F(A,B,C)=Σm(0,2,5,7) with no don’t-cares, find the minimum total literal count of a two-level SOP.',4,'Two pairs of ON cells; no allowable OFF coverage','Find the minimal sum of product literal counts.','Minimal SOP cover',r'F=\bar A\bar C+AC',r'Minterms 0,2 form ĀC̄, and minterms 5,7 form AC. | No group can join these pairs without including an OFF cell, so two two-literal products are required. | Total literal count is 2+2=4.','Count total literals across all products, not only the number of products.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'For F=AB+ĀC implemented as two AND gates into an OR, a static-1 hazard can occur when A changes with B=C=1. How many additional product terms are sufficient for its standard hazard-free cover?',1,'Two-level SOP with unequal gate delays; only A changes; B=C=1','Find the number of needed consensus products.','Consensus coverage for a static hazard',r'AB+\bar AC\ \longrightarrow\ AB+\bar AC+BC',r'The ideal function stays 1, but delay mismatch can briefly make both original products zero. | The consensus term BC is independent of the changing input and remains high for the transition. | Adding that one product suffices for this stated static-1 hazard.','Logical redundancy can be useful for transition safety even though it does not change the truth table.')
b.s(r'Which statements about don’t-care cells in minimization are correct?',['A','B','D'],['They may be assigned 1 when that enlarges a useful ON group','They may instead be assigned 0 if that helps the chosen implementation','Every don’t-care must appear in the final ON cover','A specified OFF minterm cannot be treated as a don’t-care without changing the problem'],'Ordinary incompletely specified Boolean function','Select legitimate freedoms and constraints.','Specified versus unspecified outputs',r'F(x)\text{ is unconstrained only on declared don’t-care inputs}',r'The output on a declared don’t-care input is not specified by the design requirements. | A minimizing implementation may use it as high or low; no rule forces its coverage. | Specified zero cells remain constraints. Select A,B,D.','Only explicitly declared unspecified cases may be assigned freely.')

b=Bank('topic-28','digital','combinational-circuits','Combinational Logic · Arithmetic & Selection','008');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A 4-bit unsigned adder receives A=9, B=6 and carry-in 1. Find its carry-out bit.',1,'Four-bit operands; total arithmetic sum includes Cin','Find the fifth sum bit.','Unsigned carry',r'C_{out}=\left\lfloor\frac{A+B+C_{in}}{16}\right\rfloor',r'The full sum is 9+6+1=16. | Its lower four bits are zero. | The next bit is 1, so carry-out is 1.','Carry-in must be included before deciding whether the range is exceeded.',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'In 4-bit two’s-complement addition, 7+3 produces low four bits 1010. Which flag interpretation is correct?','C',['No signed overflow because 10≤15','Both operands are negative','Signed overflow occurs although unsigned carry-out is zero','The exact signed result is −6 without overflow'],'Signed range −8…7; operands +7,+3','Distinguish signed overflow from carry.','Two’s-complement overflow',r'V=\bar{(a_s\oplus b_s)}(a_s\oplus s_s)',r'Two positive operands mathematically sum to 10, beyond signed maximum 7. | The retained word 1010 decodes to −6, revealing signed overflow. | Unsigned sum 10 is below 16, so no carry-out is produced. Select C.','Carry-out and signed overflow report different range failures.')
b.n(r'A 4-bit two’s-complement subtractor computes 3−5. Find the retained unsigned bit-pattern value in decimal (0 to 15).',14,'Four-bit modular result; mathematical signed difference −2','Find −2 modulo 16.','Modular subtraction encoding',r'(-2)\bmod16=14',r'The arithmetic difference is −2. | Its 4-bit two’s-complement encoding is 16−2=14. | Thus the retained bits are 1110, whose unsigned pattern value is 14.','The requested pattern value is unsigned even though the subtraction result is signed.')
b.n(r'A 3-to-8 active-high decoder is enabled and receives input 110₂. Find the index of its asserted output line, numbered 0 to 7.',6,'Binary-coded decoder input; conventional output indexing','Find the selected minterm index.','Decoder addressing',r'index=4A_2+2A_1+A_0',r'Convert the input word 110 to decimal: 4+2+0=6. | A one-hot decoder asserts the line matching that index. | Its high output is Y6.','An enabled active-high decoder produces one high output for a valid stable input.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A priority encoder has priority D3>D2>D1>D0. Inputs D3D2D1D0=0110 are applied. Find the selected index as a decimal value.',2,'Highest asserted input has priority; lines 2 and 1 are high','Find the highest active input index.','Priority selection',r'index=\max\{i:D_i=1\}',r'D3 is low, so it is not selected. | D2 and D1 are high; D2 has higher priority. | The selected index is 2, encoded as 10₂.','Priority encoders do not assume only one input can be asserted.')
b.n(r'An even-parity bit is appended to data 1011. What parity bit value is required?',1,'Data contains three ones; total including parity must be even','Choose the appended parity bit.','Even parity',r'p=\bigoplus_i d_i',r'The data has an odd count of ones. | Appending 1 makes the total count four. | The required parity bit is therefore 1.','The parity convention is even, so an odd data population requires a high parity bit.')
b.s(r'A full adder is constructed using two half adders and an OR. With U=A XOR B, which relations are correct?',['A','B','D'],['S=U XOR Cin','Cout=AB OR (U AND Cin)','Cout=U XOR Cin','The two half-adder carry terms are ORed'],'Conventional two-half-adder realization','Select valid intermediate/output relations.','Full-adder decomposition',r'S=A\oplus B\oplus C_{in},\quad C_{out}=AB+(A\oplus B)C_{in}',r'The first half adder gives U=A XOR B and carry AB. | The second adds U and Cin, giving sum U XOR Cin and carry U·Cin. | ORing both carry terms gives Cout. Select A,B,D.','The sum bit from the second half adder is not the full-adder carry bit.')
b.n(r'A 4:1 mux realizes F=A XOR B XOR C using select lines A,B in that order and data order D0…D3 for 00,01,10,11. How many data inputs must equal NOT C?',2,'Select variables A,B; remaining variable C','Count complemented-C cofactors.','Multiplexer function synthesis',r'F|_{AB}=C,\bar C,\bar C,C',r'When A,B have even parity (00 or 11), output must equal C. | When they have odd parity (01 or 10), output must be C̄. | Thus two data inputs, D1 and D2, are NOT C.','Use the explicitly stated mux select ordering.')
b.n(r'A 3-bit equality comparator has independent uniformly random input words A and B. Find the probability its equality output is high.',0.125,'Eight equally likely words per independent input','Find P(A=B).','Comparator matching count',r'P(A=B)=8/(8\cdot8)',r'There are 64 equally likely ordered input-word pairs. | For each of the eight A words exactly one B word matches. | Probability is 8/64=1/8.','Each input is a three-bit word, not one Boolean input.')
b.m(r'Why does an ordinary binary priority encoder often include a valid-output flag?','B',['To make every input simultaneous','To distinguish no asserted input from the valid lowest-priority index 0','To make the output analog','To remove all combinational delay'],'Output binary code can be zero both when no input is active and when D0 alone is active','Identify the ambiguity resolved by valid.','Encoder validity',r'valid=\bigvee_i D_i',r'Binary index 0 is a legitimate code for the lowest input. | If no input is asserted, a default binary output of 0 would be indistinguishable without another signal. | The OR-derived valid flag resolves that distinction, option B.','An all-zero index code does not itself establish that an input is active.')
b.n(r'For a 4-bit carry-lookahead adder, define Pi=Ai XOR Bi and Gi=AiBi. Let (P0,G0)=(1,0),(P1,G1)=(1,0),(P2,G2)=(0,1),(P3,G3)=(1,0), and C0=0. Find C4.',1,'Bit numbering starts at least-significant bit 0; valid propagate/generate pairs','Propagate the internally generated carry.','Carry lookahead recurrence',r'C_{i+1}=G_i+P_iC_i',r'C1=0 and C2=0 because the low bits propagate an initial zero carry. | Bit 2 generates C3=1 regardless of its input carry. | Bit 3 propagates that carry, so C4=1.','Carry generation at an internal bit can determine carry-out even when carry-in is zero.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'Using only 2-input AND gates with arbitrary fanout and no other gate type, find the minimum gate count to compute the AND of six independent input variables.',5,'Six distinct inputs; fan-in limited to 2; no complemented logic primitives','Find the minimum binary reduction network size.','Fan-in reduction bound',r'N_{gates}=n-1',r'A two-input gate can reduce the number of independently combined groups by at most one. | Six singleton input groups must be combined into one, requiring at least five merges. | A five-gate tree achieves it, so the minimum is 5.','Fanout does not increase the number of independent variables a two-input reduction can merge per gate.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-29','digital','sequential-circuits','Sequential Logic · State Machines & Timing','008');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'How many flip-flops are minimally required to encode 10 distinct finite-state-machine states in binary?',4,'Ten states; ordinary fixed binary encoding','Find the smallest n with 2ⁿ≥10.','State encoding capacity',r'n=\lceil\log_2M\rceil',r'Three bits encode only eight states, fewer than ten. | Four bits encode sixteen states. | The smallest adequate count is 4.','Unused binary patterns are allowed in a minimal-width state encoding.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A synchronous 3-bit up-counter starts at 110₂ and increments modulo 8 on each rising edge. Find the decimal state after 3 edges.',1,'Initial state 6; three modulo-8 increments','Find (6+3) mod8.','Modulo counter evolution',r'q_k=(q_0+k)\bmod8',r'The states after successive edges are 111,000,001. | This wraps through zero on the second edge. | The third state is 001, decimal 1.','The modulus comes from the three-bit state width.')
b.n(r'A T flip-flop starts at Q=0. Its T values at three active edges are 1,0,1. Find the final Q.',0,'Initial state zero; toggle-enable sequence 1,0,1','Track every active-edge transition.','T flip-flop update',r'Q^+=Q\oplus T',r'The first high T toggles Q to 1. | T=0 on the second edge preserves 1. | The last high T toggles it back to 0.','A low T preserves the state rather than resetting it.',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'A latch is enabled whenever its gate signal is high. While the gate remains high, its ideal Q follows D. This device is:','A',['Level sensitive','Positive-edge triggered only','A combinational NOT gate','A counter by itself'],'Transparent behavior during an enable level','Classify the storage element.','Latch versus flip-flop',r'Q=D\quad\text{while enable is active}',r'Transparency persists throughout the active level, not only at one instant. | That is the defining ideal level-sensitive latch behavior. | Option A is correct; an edge-triggered flip-flop samples only near its active edge.','Do not substitute edge-triggered timing assumptions for a transparent latch.')
b.n(r'A serial shift register starts Q3Q2Q1Q0=1001. On each edge, Q3←Q2, Q2←Q1, Q1←Q0, Q0←Din. With Din=0 on two edges, find the final word as an unsigned decimal.',4,'Four-bit register; explicit simultaneous next-state wiring; two zero input bits','Apply both shifts using old-state values.','Synchronous shift-register updates',r'(Q_3,Q_2,Q_1,Q_0)^+=(Q_2,Q_1,Q_0,D_{in})',r'After the first edge 1001 becomes 0010. | After the second edge it becomes 0100. | The final unsigned value is 4.','All flip-flops update from the previous state simultaneously; do not ripple in-place through the assignment list.')
b.n(r'A synchronous FSM has states 0,1,2 and transition qnext=(q+1) mod3 independent of input. Starting at 2, find its state after 5 clock edges.',1,'Modulo-3 transition; initial state 2','Apply five transitions.','Finite-state transition iteration',r'q_k=(q_0+k)\bmod3',r'Five updates add 5 to the initial index 2. | Reduce 7 modulo 3. | The state is 1.','State labels here are defined numerically by the transition rule; no unused fourth codeword is visited.')
b.s(r'Which statements about synchronous sequential circuits are correct?',['A','C','D'],['Flip-flops use a common logical capture clock in the ideal synchronous model','Combinational logic can never lie between two registers','Setup constraints limit maximum path delay for a chosen clock period','Hold constraints involve earliest new-data arrival after a capture edge'],'Ordinary edge-triggered synchronous circuit; ideal common clock model','Select valid timing/model descriptions.','Register-to-register timing',r'T_{clk}\ge t_{cq,max}+t_{comb,max}+t_{setup}',r'A common capture-clock abstraction defines synchronous state updates. | Combinational functions commonly connect register outputs to later register inputs. | Setup checks the latest arrival before the next edge, while hold checks the earliest post-edge change. Select A,C,D.','Setup and hold constrain different delay extremes.')
b.n(r'A setup-limited path has tcq,max=0.8 ns, combinational delay 3 ns, setup 0.2 ns and zero skew. Find the maximum clock frequency in MHz.',250,'Total timing path components in ns','Convert minimum period into frequency.','Synchronous maximum clock rate',r'f_{max}=1/(t_{cq,max}+t_{comb,max}+t_{setup})',r'The minimum clock period is 0.8+3+0.2=4 ns. | Its reciprocal is 1/(4×10⁻⁹)=250×10⁶ Hz. | The maximum setup-limited frequency is 250 MHz, assuming hold and other constraints also pass.','The reciprocal of a nanosecond value is in gigahertz before conversion.',unit='MHz')
b.m(r'A modulo-6 synchronous counter uses a 3-bit encoding and visits exactly six valid states. How many of the eight binary patterns remain unused?','C',['0','1','2','6'],'Three storage bits; exactly six reachable intended states','Count unused encodings.','Encoding slack',r'N_{unused}=2^3-6',r'Three bits provide eight encodings. | Six are assigned to the intended state cycle. | Two remain unused; their recovery behavior still needs a design decision.','Unused states are possible even in a minimum-width encoding.')
b.n(r'A Moore-machine output is y=1 only in state S2. Transitions under input 1 cycle S0→S1→S2→S0. Starting in S0 and applying four 1 inputs at successive clocks, find y after the fourth clock.',0,'Moore output depends on current state; cyclic transitions on input1','Track state then evaluate output.','Moore timing convention',r'y_k=g(q_k)',r'The state sequence after clocks is S1,S2,S0,S1. | After the fourth clock the machine is in S1, not S2. | Thus output y=0.','For a Moore machine evaluate the state reached at the requested time, not the next input directly.')
b.s(r'Which statements distinguish ordinary Moore and Mealy FSM output models?',['A','B','D'],['A Moore output is a function of state alone','A Mealy output can depend on current state and current input','A Mealy output is forbidden from ever changing between clock edges','A Moore output may change when its encoded state changes'],'Conventional combinational output logic with synchronous state storage','Select correct output dependencies.','FSM output definitions',r'y_{Moore}=g(q),\quad y_{Mealy}=g(q,x)',r'Moore and Mealy models differ in whether input appears in the output function. | A Mealy combinational output may respond to an input change before the next state clock. | A Moore output can change when state storage changes; hence A,B,D hold. Real implementations may register outputs for additional timing control.','The abstract dependency model does not by itself guarantee a glitch-free physical output.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'Two positive-edge registers use the same period T with capture clock arriving 0.3 ns later than the launch clock. Let tcq,max=1 ns, combinational delay=4 ns and setup=0.5 ns. Ignoring all other uncertainty, find the minimum setup-limited T in ns.',5.2,'Positive capture skew +0.3 ns; max path 1+4 ns; setup 0.5 ns','Include clock skew with the correct sign.','Setup with useful capture skew',r'T\ge t_{cq,max}+t_{comb,max}+t_{setup}-t_{skew}',r'The capture edge occurs at T+0.3 ns relative to the launch edge. | Data arrives at 1+4=5 ns and must precede that edge by 0.5 ns. | Thus 5+0.5≤T+0.3, giving T≥5.2 ns. Hold impact must be checked separately.','A later capture clock helps setup but can worsen hold.',unit='ns',difficulty='hard')
save_batch(banks,'batch-008')
